A struct declares a nominal runtime type with named fields.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Vector2 = |
        x F64,
        y F64 = 0.0,
    |
]

### Contract for struct declarations

- The declaration form is `Name = | fields |`.
- Struct names use `PascalCase`.
- Every field has a name and explicit type.
- A field keeps its exact declared type. `I32` and `U32` remain distinct even when their physical widths match, as do `Byte` and `U8`.
- Field names must be unique.
- A field may provide `= default`.
- Every field default must fully evaluate at compile time.
- A field without a default is required during construction.
- Matching field shapes do not make two structs interchangeable.
- A transparent type alias does not create a new struct identity.

Field types remain concrete declaration facts. Constructors and defaults check
values against those exact types; later uses never retag a field. Generic
nominal shapes follow the bounded numeric-origin rules in
@../generics (Generics).

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Point = |
        x I32,
        y I32,
    |

    Position = |
        x I32,
        y I32,
    |
]

`Point` and `Position` remain different types.

### Generic structs

Struct declarations may introduce declaration-site generic parameters.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Box type A = |
        value A,
    |
]

Generic construction and inference are documented by @../generics (Generics).
